body:
Gunungkidul, May 1, 2026 – Plant physiology testing activities using the LI-COR 6800 Portable Photosynthesis System were conducted over three consecutive days from April 29 to May 1, 2026 in the KTH Watu Lawang area, Gunungkidul, Special Region of Yogyakarta. This activity is part of collaborative research with researchers from BRIN's Biological Research and Environmental Organization in supporting the development of data-driven agroforestry systems and environmental sustainability.

Testing was conducted on several plant commodities, namely jackfruit, legumes, eucalyptus, and corn, to determine the physiological condition and health of plants directly in the field. Data collection was performed using modern photosynthesis measurement technology capable of recording important plant parameters such as photosynthetic rate, stomatal conductance, and plant water use efficiency in real-time.

In this activity, the equipment operation technical team consisted of Samuel Gatot Marseno, S.T.P., Fathuzaky Setyawan, S.T.P., and Fadel Arya Perdana S.Si. who were responsible for equipment preparation, sensor calibration, field data collection, and ensuring research data quality remained optimal throughout the activity.

![](/brand/uploads/notes/809-1-image-8-1030x773.webp)

The use of plant physiology measurement technology such as LI-COR 6800 has become an important part in supporting the development of precision agriculture and sustainable agroforestry. This approach aligns with the development of smart agricultural research that is continuously being promoted by various research institutions and universities to improve efficiency in data-driven plant management.

![](/brand/uploads/notes/809-2-image-9-edited.webp)

Beyond focusing on the scientific aspect, this research is also directed at supporting the development of agroforestry areas that have ecological and economic value for the surrounding community. Through monitoring plant health conditions, the research results are expected to serve as a basis for creating agroforestry areas that are more attractive, productive, and adaptive to environmental changes. Development of healthy and well-managed agroforestry landscapes also has the potential to increase nature-based tourism appeal in the community forest area of Gunungkidul.

SDG 2: Zero Hunger, through strengthening sustainable agriculture systems and plant productivity;

SDG 9: Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure, through the application of modern technology in agricultural research;

SDG 13: Climate Action, through agroforestry management that is adaptive to climate change;

SDG 15: Life on Land, through efforts to maintain vegetation health and sustainability of terrestrial ecosystems;

SDG 17: Partnerships for the Goals, through research collaboration between universities, research institutions, and communities

original.body:
Gunungkidul, 1 Mei 2026 – Kegiatan pengujian fisiologi tanaman menggunakan alat LI-COR 6800 Portable Photosynthesis System telah dilaksanakan selama tiga hari berturut-turut pada tanggal 29 April hingga 1 Mei 2026 di kawasan KTH Watu Lawang, Gunungkidul, Daerah Istimewa Yogyakarta. Kegiatan ini merupakan bagian dari penelitian kolaboratif bersama peneliti dari Organisasi Riset Hayati dan Lingkungan BRIN dalam mendukung pengembangan sistem agroforestri berbasis data dan keberlanjutan lingkungan.

Pengujian dilakukan pada beberapa komoditas tanaman, yaitu nangka, kacang, kayu putih, dan jagung, untuk mengetahui kondisi fisiologis dan kesehatan tanaman secara langsung di lapangan. Pengambilan data dilakukan menggunakan teknologi pengukuran fotosintesis modern yang mampu merekam parameter penting tanaman seperti laju fotosintesis, konduktansi stomata, hingga efisiensi penggunaan air tanaman secara real-time.

Dalam kegiatan ini, tim teknisi pengoperasian alat terdiri atas Samuel Gatot Marseno, S.T.P., Fathuzaky Setyawan, S.T.P., dan Fadel Arya Perdana S.Si. yang bertugas melakukan persiapan alat, kalibrasi sensor, pengambilan data lapangan, serta memastikan kualitas data penelitian tetap optimal selama kegiatan berlangsung.

![](/brand/uploads/notes/809-1-image-8-1030x773.webp)

Penggunaan teknologi pengukuran fisiologi tanaman seperti LI-COR 6800 menjadi bagian penting dalam mendukung pengembangan pertanian presisi dan agroforestri berkelanjutan. Pendekatan ini sejalan dengan pengembangan riset pertanian cerdas yang terus didorong oleh berbagai institusi riset dan perguruan tinggi untuk meningkatkan efisiensi pengelolaan tanaman berbasis data ilmiah.  

![](/brand/uploads/notes/809-2-image-9-edited.webp)

Selain berfokus pada aspek ilmiah, penelitian ini juga diarahkan untuk mendukung pengembangan kawasan agroforestri yang memiliki nilai ekologis dan ekonomi bagi masyarakat sekitar. Melalui pemantauan kondisi kesehatan tanaman, hasil penelitian diharapkan mampu menjadi dasar dalam menciptakan kawasan agroforestri yang lebih menarik, produktif, dan adaptif terhadap perubahan lingkungan. Pengembangan lanskap agroforestri yang sehat dan terkelola dengan baik juga berpotensi meningkatkan daya tarik wisata berbasis alam di kawasan hutan rakyat Gunungkidul.

SDG 2: Zero Hunger, melalui penguatan sistem pertanian berkelanjutan dan produktivitas tanaman;

SDG 9: Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure, melalui penerapan teknologi modern dalam penelitian pertanian;

SDG 13: Climate Action, melalui pengelolaan agroforestri yang adaptif terhadap perubahan iklim;

SDG 15: Life on Land, melalui upaya menjaga kesehatan vegetasi dan keberlanjutan ekosistem daratan;

SDG 17: Partnerships for the Goals, melalui kolaborasi riset antara perguruan tinggi, lembaga penelitian, dan masyarakat
